<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1200 BCE · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Gayatri Mantra</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="sa">गायत्री मन्त्र</bdi> / <bdi lang="sa-Latn">Gāyatrī Mantra</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A short prayer to the sun from the Rigveda, one of the oldest Hindu texts, chanted every day by many Hindus.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>attributed to Vishvamitra</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Vedic Hindus; Hindus in India, Indonesia and beyond</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit (Vedic)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>not known (Rigveda, Mandala 3)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>Rigveda 3.62.10 (Rigvedic period; dates debated)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (Rigvedic verse attributed to the sage Vishvamitra (ancient))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The Gāyatrī Mantra, also called the Sāvitrī Mantra, is a verse of the Rigveda (Mandala 3, hymn 62, verse 10) addressed to Savitr, a Vedic solar deity. It is attributed to the sage Viśvāmitra, who is also regarded as the author of the whole third book of the Rigveda. Its name comes from its metre, the gāyatrī, three lines of eight syllables; as preserved, its first line is one syllable short, and reciters either lengthen a sound or use a reconstructed form to fill it. In recitation the verse is preceded by the syllable oṃ and the three &quot;great utterances&quot;, the mahāvyāhṛti, a practice set out in the Taittiriya Aranyaka. Gāyatrī is also the name of the goddess of the mantra and the metre.</p><p>The verse is cited throughout Hindu literature: in the Brahmanas and Srauta-sutras of the Vedic liturgy, in Upanishads such as the Brihadaranyaka, Shvetashvatara and Maitrayaniya, which treat it esoterically, and in the Mahabharata, the Harivamsa and the Manusmṛti. It served as the model for later &quot;gāyatrīs&quot; addressed to other gods and goddesses, built on the same pattern. The Buddha knew it: in the Pali canon he calls the Sāvittī the foremost of metres, and uses it as a sign of Brahmanic learning.</p><p>For centuries the mantra was bound to the upanayana, the ceremony that begins a young man's study of the Vedas; Sarvepalli Radhakrishnan called its imparting the essence of the rite, which is sometimes named &quot;Gayatri diksha&quot;. Traditionally this verse was given only to Brahmins, with other verses for Kshatriyas and Vaishyas. Its repetition (japa) has been used as a form of atonement, and practitioners hold that it brings wisdom through the Sun.</p><p>Modern reform movements opened it to all. In 1827 Ram Mohan Roy published a dissertation on the mantra; from 1830 members of the Brahmo Samaj used it in private devotion, and the First Covenant of 1843 required it for worship. In 1898 Swami Vivekananda, arguing that one became a Brahmin by learning rather than by birth, gave the mantra to non-Brahmins in the Ramakrishna Mission, and Dayananda Saraswati, founder of the Arya Samaj, gave it a rendering of his own. In the later twentieth century the All World Gayatri Pariwar spread it further through gatherings large and small, and it has reached the wider public on pendants and recordings. In Indonesia it opens the Trisandhyā Puja, the prayer that Balinese and many other Indonesian Hindus say three times a day, at 6 a.m., noon and 6 p.m.</p><p>Western scholars translated it from the early nineteenth century, among them William Jones (1807), Monier Monier-Williams (1882) and Ralph T.H. Griffith (1896). It has since appeared in popular culture: engraved on the belt of the statue of George Harrison unveiled in Liverpool in 2015, in the opening theme of the television series Battlestar Galactica (2004), and sung by Cher on her tours from 2002.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A Vedic prayer addressed to Savitr, the god who stirs and quickens all things through the sun's light. The worshippers ask to share in his radiance and that he awaken their minds and understanding. It is said as a daily act of devotion and as a seal of learning, in a mood of quiet reverence.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Sanskrit</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Sanskrit</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तत्सवितुर्वरेण्यं भर्गो देवस्य धीमहि ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tatsaviturvareṇyaṃ bhargo devasya dhīmahi |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That desirable radiance of the god Savitr may we meditate on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">धियो यो नः प्रचोदयात् ॥१०॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">dhiyo yo naḥ pracodayāt ||10||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who shall spur on our thoughts. (10)</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Rigveda 3.62.10. Text from <a href="https://sa.wikisource.org/wiki/%E0%A4%8B%E0%A4%97%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%B5%E0%A5%87%E0%A4%A6%E0%A4%83_%E0%A4%B8%E0%A5%82%E0%A4%95%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%A4%E0%A4%82_%E0%A5%A9.%E0%A5%AC%E0%A5%A8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (IAST).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: dhīmahi is disputed. Grammatically it is most often taken from dhā- 'to place, receive' ('may we receive / take to ourselves'); the traditional reading takes it from dhī- 'to think' ('may we meditate on'). Either can be printed; the editor may prefer 'may we meditate on' as the sense most readers know.</li><li>line 1.2: yo (masculine 'who') refers to Savitr, the god, not to bhargas (neuter 'radiance'). dhiyaḥ: thoughts, insights, also prayers / poetic visions. pracodayāt: 'may he impel, urge on' (optative-like subjunctive).</li><li>Savitr (Savitṛ), 'the Impeller', a solar god of the Rigveda; kept as a name. vareṇya: 'to be chosen, choice, excellent'.</li><li>The text is the bare Rigveda verse 3.62.10, without the oṃ bhūr bhuvaḥ svaḥ that is prefixed in recitation; romanized with the sandhi joins as written (tatsaviturvareṇyaṃ = tat savitur vareṇyaṃ). The verse number ||10|| is kept.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="29-A4Yaz07c" data-title="Gayatri Mantra: Sri K. Suresh, chanted in the Krishna Yajurveda tradition"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Sri K. Suresh, chanted in the Krishna Yajurveda tradition</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Ghanapati. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=29-A4Yaz07c" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Derivative gāyatrī mantras to other deities (Vishnu, Shiva, Ganesha, Durga, Lakshmi and others)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same metre and formula</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lily (Kate Bush, album The Red Shoes)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1993</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a variation on W. Q. Judge's translation used as the introduction</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mohabbatein (film, Aditya Chopra)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2000</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recited by Amitabh Bachchan</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Living Proof: The Farewell Tour (Cher)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2002</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung on stage; reprised in later shows to 2020</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Battlestar Galactica (television series)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2004</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a version in the opening theme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Statue of George Harrison, Liverpool</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2015</span><span class="sg-chain-h">engraved on the belt</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Homeworld: Deserts of Kharak (video game)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2016</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in the final mission</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The White Lotus (HBO)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2021</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung by a character in the first season</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Empyrean (Schammasch, album Triangle)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as the outro, as a Gregorian chant</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pantheon (television series)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">modern remix in the background</span></li></ul></section>
